Patch summary:
Encode the ASTC textures decoded on the CPU into BC3 blocks, a quarter of
their size as RGBA8. With CPU decoding (Mesa's Dozen on WSL2), every ASTC
texture became an RGBA8 output image of 4 bytes per texel: 3DMark Steel
Nomad Light's 105 textures were 6.1 GB (1549 Mpix) of GPU-PV allocations in
host memory, which on a 32 GB host with a 15 GB WSL VM left Windows 646 MB
free when its 2-second GPU watchdog fired (LiveKernelEvent 141), Dozen lost
the D3D12 device and gfxstream aborted the VM. D3D12 has no ASTC but every
D3D12 device samples BC formats natively.

- With CPU decoding, gfxstream's GPU decoder is set to its ASTC-to-BC3
  transcoder (AstcDecoder::NewBc3, upstream code that no caller selected),
  so an emulated ASTC image's output image is BC3 (sRGB for sRGB ASTC), its
  memory a quarter of RGBA8's in the guest too, and the GPU decoder, which
  decodes what the CPU cannot, writes the same format.
- AstcTexture gets the output format: for BC3 it decodes each region's
  layer into RGBA8 scratch memory, encodes it into 4x4 BC3 blocks with
  stb_dxt (the texels of a block past the level's edge repeat the edge, up
  to 8 threads for large levels), and stages and copies tightly packed
  blocks. An ASTC block-aligned region that starts or ends inside a 4x4
  block (an upload of part of a 6x6 level from x = 6) cannot be encoded
  without the texels outside the copy; such a copy, like the others the
  CPU cannot decode, is decoded on the GPU.
- Whether that GPU fallback exists is probed once per device: the GPU
  decoder's output image is a BC3 image with storage usage and
  block-texel-view-compatible flags, viewed as R32G32B32A32_UINT; D3D12
  refuses the unordered-access flag on a BC resource unless the driver
  supports relaxed format casting, which a format query does not show (an
  Intel UHD 770 under WSL2 reports an impossible memory size for the image
  and its allocation fails). Where the probe fails, ASTC images are created
  as plain BC3 images and data the CPU cannot decode stays undecoded, with
  a warning naming the image. Steel Nomad Light's uploads are all decoded
  on the CPU.
- An image-to-image copy from another ASTC image the CPU decodes copies the
  two output images as well, with the same regions, when the source's output
  is decoded, the output formats match and, for BC3, the regions are aligned
  to its 4x4 blocks in both images; the destination then stays decoded
  without the GPU decoder, which a host without the fallback does not have.
  Other copies still fall back to the GPU decoder.
- IKA_ASTC_DECODE=cpu-rgba8 decodes on the CPU into RGBA8 images, as before
  BC3, for comparisons; the GPU fallback then needs no probe.
- The decoding log line adds the staged bytes ("... MB of BC3 blocks").
- stb_dxt.h v1.12 (public domain) joins the bundled stb library.
- Unit tests of the BC3 staging layout, of the rejected unaligned regions,
  and of the blocks decoding back to the texels, solid and from
  astc-encoder's checkerboard.
